var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"api/#API","page":"API","text":"","title":"API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#The-problem","page":"API","text":"A problem holds functions and nothing else: the residual, and optionally its derivative. The storage of the iterate selects the call form of both, so one problem serves every regime. This name is exported.","title":"The problem"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#The-storage-seam","page":"API","text":"The storage seam lets an algorithm read each function's result rather than the storage itself, so the storage-specific operations stay at the leaves. Each function has a value method for a StaticArray or a Number iterate (R1) and an in-place method for an AbstractArray buffer slot (R3).\n\nThese names are not exported. Reach them as GeometricSolvers.name.","title":"The storage seam"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Status-and-stopping-test","page":"API","text":"Every type on this page is isbits, so a kernel can hold it, and every real field is in R = real(T) for the element type T of the iterate.","title":"Status and stopping test"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Linear-solver-methods","page":"API","text":"The factorisation precision TF of a linear-solver method is a type parameter. Nothing selects the working type of the problem, a real float type a lower (or higher) precision, and a marker a vendor tensor-core path.","title":"Linear-solver methods"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Factorisation-and-solve","page":"API","text":"A linear-solver method enters a solve through dense_factor, factorize!! and ldiv!!. For a static matrix (R1) factorize!! and ldiv!! return new isbits values, so they run inside a kernel: StaticLU is LU with partial pivoting and Int32 pivots, StaticQR Householder QR without column pivoting, and StaticSVD a one-sided Jacobi singular value decomposition whose solve truncates at the rank cutoff √n · eps(TF) · σ₁, which is what makes a rank-revealing solve finite. For a vector state (R3) dense_factor pre-allocates DenseLU, DenseQR or DenseSVD, and factorize!! and ldiv!! reuse its buffers in place: through LAPACK on a host Array, and through KernelAbstractions on an AbstractGPUArray for DenseLU, DenseQR and DenseSVD. factorize!! states what a singular matrix gives: an SVD factor records its numerical rank in rank apart from its status, and exact_solve tells Newton whether the solve is exact or truncated; singular_values gives the singular values of a StaticSVD. These names are not exported and are reached as GeometricSolvers.name.","title":"Factorisation and solve"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Eigensolver","page":"API","text":"jacobi_eigen is a function on a matrix and not a linear-solver method: cyclic two-sided Jacobi for a Hermitian or a real symmetric matrix, on the rotations and the round-robin ordering of the Jacobi SVD with a Frobenius-norm pair floor eps(R) * ‖H‖_F, for the case jacobi_eigen(im * M) of a real skew M that the ProjectedSkew exponential algorithm of GeometricOptimizers needs. It adds no method to LinearAlgebra.eigen, because a method of eigen for a StaticArrays type would be type piracy.","title":"Eigensolver"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Line-searches","page":"API","text":"A line search is scalar code: linesearch sees the line only through the merit φ and its derivative φ′, allocates nothing and never throws, so the same search runs on the host and inside a kernel. Its iteration cap, where it has one, is its own field. Along a LineFunction its LineSearchResult also carries the residual at the step, so the caller does not evaluate it again.\n\nA trust region is the other globalization of Newton. DogLeg is no line search: it needs the Jacobian, its factor and a radius that lives across iterations, so Newton keeps the radius in its state and runs the trial loop on the dogleg curve itself.","title":"Line searches"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Nonlinear-solvers","page":"API","text":"A nonlinear solver holds its algorithmic configuration (linsolve, globalization, jacobian, refinement, regularization and reuse for Newton; damping and globalization for Picard) and nothing about the problem or the iterate. solve adapts the method to R = real(eltype(x₀)), calls init_state and solve!! on a copy of x₀ when x₀ is an array, and returns (x, status). With the same arguments, solver = init(prob, x₀, method, p; options, kwargs...) pre-allocates a NonlinearSolver that holds a copy of an array x₀ and p, solve!(solver) solves into solver.x and returns (solver.x, status), and solve!(solver, x, p) solves from another x with other p of the same types, in place into an array x. init, solve! and solve are the functions of CommonSolve.jl, which the package extends on its own types, and solve!(init(args...)) returns what solve(args...) returns. solve!! takes a solver state from init_state(method_R, prob, x₀, p) and runs the loop in R1 and R3 alike. On a batch X of independent small systems, solve_batch! solves each in place on get_backend(X) with one work-item per system, and PerSystem wraps an indexable collection of per-system parameters so system i receives p.data[i].","title":"Nonlinear solvers"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Automatic-differentiation","page":"API","text":"In R1, prepare_ad returns the AutoForwardDiff() back end itself for a StaticVector or a Number iterate. jacobian!! and jvp!! run tagged ForwardDiff directly with that source. The source nothing calls the problem's own J; a JacobianFunction calls a method's operator for jacobian!! only. A back end other than AutoForwardDiff has no R1 method, and the call is a host MethodError.\n\nIn R3 the AD path is chosen once, by prepare_ad, from the back end and the storage of the iterate: AutoForwardDiff() goes through an own chunked forward mode on every array type, and any other back end goes through DifferentiationInterface on a CPU iterate, so every ADTypes back end works with no line of source here. A device array iterate takes AutoForwardDiff() only, because DifferentiationInterface's jacobian! raises a scalar-indexing error on an MtlArray, a CuArray and a JLArray. When the problem supplies an analytic 3-argument J(Jbuf, x, p), JacobianMatrixJVP evaluates prob.J into its pre-allocated matrix buffer and multiplies by the direction in place. Only a LineSearch other than Static takes a JVP, so under any other globalization the analytic J gets no JVP preparation and no second n × n buffer. jacobian!! and jvp!! then dispatch on the object the preparation returned, so the solver makes no runtime choice and carries no ad keyword.\n\nIn R3 the iterate is a vector; another shape raises ArgumentError where the preparation is built. The parameters may be replaced on every call, which is what lets one preparation serve a whole time-stepping loop.\n\nAutoEnzyme reaches the Array path through DifferentiationInterface like any other back end, but it needs runtime activity for the parameter context — AutoEnzyme(; mode = Enzyme.set_runtime_activity(Enzyme.Forward)). A plain AutoEnzyme() raises EnzymeRuntimeActivityError on a residual that broadcasts constant parameters into its result; see KNOWN_ISSUES.md. The mode is the caller's choice, so the package sets none.\n\nThese names are not exported. Reach them as GeometricSolvers.name.","title":"Automatic differentiation"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Internals","page":"API","text":"These names are not exported. Reach them as GeometricSolvers.name.\n\nA nonlinear method enters the loop of solve!! through init_state, which builds its state, step!!, and the two hooks start!! and finish!!, which run once before the first step and at every return. residual_buffer names the slot of the state that holds F(x₀).","title":"Internals"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.NonlinearProblem","page":"API","text":"NonlinearProblem(F)\nNonlinearProblem(F, J)\n\nThe residual F of a nonlinear system, and optionally its derivative J. The problem holds functions only — no parameters, no iterate and no flag — so it is isbits when F and J are and a kernel can hold it.\n\nThe call form follows the storage of the iterate, and residual!! selects it on the storage alone: with no buffer slot the call is F(x, p), for a Number, an MVector — which counts as a value — and an Array alike. J is the derivative of F, which jvp!! and jacobian!! read for the source nothing; AutoForwardDiff instead differentiates F. A method's own operator is a separate Jacobian source.\n\njulia> prob = NonlinearProblem((x, p) -> x .* x .- 4);\n\njulia> prob.F(3.0, nothing)\n5.0\n\njulia> prob.J === nothing\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.NonlinearProblem"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.buffer","page":"API","text":"buffer(x)\nbuffer(x, TF, dims...)\n\nOne storage slot for an iterate like x, in the element type TF and with the shape dims. A caller creates slots during initialization; an algorithm consumes their returned values.\n\nA value iterate — a StaticArray or a Number — has no slot: the call returns nothing, so the R1 state carries no buffer and spends no register on one. A static matrix is never an iterate and gets no slot either, which is why this method is written on StaticArray and not on StaticVector. An array iterate allocates a storage slot via similar.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.buffer"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.residual!!","page":"API","text":"residual!!(y, prob, x, p)\n\nThe residual F(x, p) of prob at the iterate x, for the parameters p, in the storage of y, and return it.\n\ny is the storage slot the residual is to occupy, and it alone selects the call form of the problem's F: with no buffer slot (nothing) the call is F(x, p), for an SVector, an MVector, a Number and an Array alike, because the iterate's type is read nowhere; with an array buffer slot y the call is F(y, x, p). There is no inplace flag that could disagree with the iterate, and no hasmethod.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.residual!!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.update_iterate!!","page":"API","text":"update_iterate!!(y, x, α, d)\n\nThe updated iterate x + α d, in the storage of y, and return it.\n\nThis is the one function that moves the iterate, and the one seam a manifold solver extends: a retraction is a second method of it, and no algorithm changes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.update_iterate!!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.map!!","page":"API","text":"map!!(f, y, xs...)\n\nmap(f, xs...) in the storage of y, and return it: the elementwise updates that are not an iterate update of update_iterate!! — a negation, a difference, a copy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.map!!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.mul!!","page":"API","text":"mul!!(C, A, B)\n\nThe matrix product A * B, in the storage of C, and return it: the Gram matrix of the Anderson and weighting histories, and a matrix–vector product where no jvp!! applies.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.mul!!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.copy!!","page":"API","text":"copy!!(dst, src)\n\nCopy src into the storage of dst, and return it. On a value slot (nothing, a StaticArray, or a Number) this returns src unchanged; on an array buffer slot it copies src into dst via copyto!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.copy!!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.scale!!","page":"API","text":"scale!!(dst, α, x)\n\nThe scaled vector α * x, in the storage of dst, and return it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.scale!!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.axpby!!","page":"API","text":"axpby!!(dst, α, x, β, y)\n\nThe linear combination α * x + β * y, in the storage of dst, and return it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.axpby!!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.regularize!!","page":"API","text":"regularize!!(J, μ)\n\nAdd μ * I to the Jacobian J in its storage, and return it. When iszero(μ) holds, J is returned untouched. On a Matrix the diagonal is shifted in a loop so bounds-checked runs allocate nothing; on any other AbstractMatrix the diagonal view view(J, diagind(J)) is updated by broadcast so device arrays perform no scalar indexing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.regularize!!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.ReturnCode","page":"API","text":"ReturnCode\n\nWhy a solve or a step ended, stored in one byte so that a status fits a GPU register budget:\n\nSUCCESS: the residual test of Options holds.\nSTALLED: the iterate or the residual stopped improving before the test held.\nMAXITERS: the iteration cap was reached.\nSINGULAR: a factorisation failed, as factorize!! states; it does not throw.\nNONFINITE: a residual, a step or a norm is not finite.\nLINESEARCH_FAILED: the globalisation found no acceptable step.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.ReturnCode"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.SolverStatus","page":"API","text":"SolverStatus{R <: Real}\n\nThe result of a solve. It is isbits, so a kernel can write one per system, and every real field is in R = real(T) for the element type T of the iterate.\n\nFields\n\ncode::ReturnCode: why the solve ended.\niterations::Int32: the number of steps taken.\nfnorm::R: F(x) at return.\nstepnorm::R: Δx of the last step.\nstep_failures::Int32: every step-rule failure of the solve, such as a failed line search, in one count.\npromoted::Bool: a factorisation ran in a higher precision than the factorisation precision of its method, after a stall or as a vendor fallback.\n\nThe predicates GeometricSolvers.isconverged and GeometricSolvers.isstalled read the code. They are not exported, because they are generic names that a package doing using GeometricSolvers may want for itself.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.SolverStatus"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.StepInfo","page":"API","text":"StepInfo{R <: Real}\n\nThe result of one step, which record folds into a SolverStatus.\n\nFields\n\ncode::ReturnCode: the outcome of the step.\nfnorm::R: F(x) at the new iterate, so that the loop needs no second reduction and, on a device, no second synchronisation.\nstepnorm::R: x - x.\nfailures::Int32: the step-rule failures inside this step, those of its members included.\npromoted::Bool: the step factorised in a higher precision than its method's.\nlimited::Bool: the step was accepted, but a trust-region radius truncated it, so its norm is set by the radius and does not measure the distance to a root.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.StepInfo"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.Options","page":"API","text":"Options{R <: Real}\n\nThe stopping test of the loop, and nothing else. It is isbits, and every tolerance is in R = real(T) for the element type T of the iterate, so a Float64 tolerance never reaches a Float32 kernel. The iteration cap of a line search is a field of the line search.\n\nFields\n\nf_abstol::R, f_reltol::R: the residual test F(x)  max(f_abstol f_reltol F(x₀)).\nx_abstol::R, x_reltol::R: the step test Δx  max(x_abstol x_reltol x).\nmin_iterations::Int32, max_iterations::Int32: the bounds on the number of steps.\nmax_stalls::Int32: the number of consecutive stalled steps after which the solve stops.\n\nOptions(T; kwargs...) builds one from the element type T of the iterate, so the caller never names R. A solve takes only an Options{R} for R = real(T) and raises an ArgumentError for any other: the tolerances of another precision do not carry over, because the Float64 default f_reltol = √eps(Float64) lies below eps(Float32) and a Float32 solve cannot reach it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.Options"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.Options-Union{Tuple{Type{T}}, Tuple{T}} where T<:Number","page":"API","text":"Options(T; f_abstol, f_reltol, x_abstol, x_reltol, min_iterations, max_iterations, max_stalls)\n\nBuild the Options of a solve whose iterate has element type T, as Options{real(T)}. The two relative tolerances scale with eps(R):\n\nkeyword default\nf_abstol 0\nf_reltol √eps(R)\nx_abstol 0\nx_reltol 2 eps(R)\nmin_iterations 0\nmax_iterations 100\nmax_stalls 2\n\nThe default residual test is relative. The residual cannot fall below about mathrmeps(T) J x, whatever the precision of the residual (Tisseur 2001, Cor. 2.5), and a default cannot know J x. So the default f_abstol is 0, which turns the absolute test off, and the relative test f_reltol F(x₀) decides.\n\nThe stopping test runs before the first step, and min_iterations is 0. From a start at the limiting accuracy one Newton step makes the result worse (Wilkinson, quoted by Tisseur 2001). The relative test cannot see such a start: before the first step it compares F(x₀) with a fraction of itself. So a start at the limiting accuracy returns unchanged after 0 iterations only when the caller sets f_abstol at or above its residual floor, as an integrator that knows the scale of F can.\n\nA negative or NaN tolerance, a negative min_iterations, a max_iterations below min_iterations and a max_stalls below 1 raise an ArgumentError.\n\njulia> opt = Options(ComplexF32; f_reltol = 1e-4);\n\njulia> typeof(opt)\nOptions{Float32}\n\njulia> opt.f_reltol\n0.0001f0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.Options"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.LUFactorization","page":"API","text":"LUFactorization()\nLUFactorization(TF)\n\nLU factorisation with partial pivoting. LUFactorization() factorises in the working type of the problem, LUFactorization(Float32) in Float32, and LUFactorization(TF32()) carries a tensor-core marker (see TF32).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.LUFactorization"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.QRFactorization","page":"API","text":"QRFactorization()\nQRFactorization(TF)\n\nHouseholder QR factorisation, with the factorisation precision of LUFactorization.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.QRFactorization"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.SVDFactorization","page":"API","text":"SVDFactorization()\nSVDFactorization(TF)\n\nSingular value decomposition, with the factorisation precision of LUFactorization.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.SVDFactorization"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.TF32","page":"API","text":"TF32()\n\nThe factorisation-precision marker for NVIDIA's TensorFloat-32 tensor cores. It is a type only: no factorisation method accepts a marker, so a marker selects no code path.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.TF32"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.FP16","page":"API","text":"FP16()\n\nThe factorisation-precision marker for half-precision tensor cores. See TF32.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.FP16"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.BF16","page":"API","text":"BF16()\n\nThe factorisation-precision marker for bfloat16 tensor cores. See TF32.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.BF16"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.dense_factor","page":"API","text":"dense_factor(method::LinearMethod, prob, x::AbstractVector, p)\n\nBuild the dense factor DenseLU, DenseQR or DenseSVD of method for the state vector x.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.dense_factor"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.factorize!!","page":"API","text":"factorize!!(fac, method, J)\n\nFactorise the matrix J with the linear-solver method method and return the factors.\n\nFor a static matrix (R1), fac is nothing or an earlier factor of the same type, whose value is not used, and the result is a new StaticLU, StaticQR or StaticSVD. J is converted to the element type of the factors, factor_eltype. Where that is not the element type of J, J is first divided by the power of two 2^e that brings its largest |re| or |im| into [1, 2), or as close as the exponent range allows, and e is kept in the factor, so that the overall scale of J neither overflows nor underflows in the factor type. A zero or non-finite J gives e = 0. A singular J does not throw. LU records the first zero pivot in info, QR the first zero diagonal entry of R, and returncode then gives SINGULAR. QR is not rank-revealing: a singular J whose R has no exact zero on the diagonal gives SUCCESS, and two equal rows, as a rule, give no exact zero. The SVD records its numerical rank in rank, the number of singular values above the cutoff √n · eps(TF) · σ₁, and a rank deficiency gives info == 0 and SUCCESS; its info is nonzero only where the sweep did not converge or a singular value is not finite, and rank is then 0. The SVD is prescaled at the working precision too, because the sweep squares the entries.\n\nFor a dense factor (R3), fac is a DenseLU, DenseQR or DenseSVD built by dense_factor. J is written into fac.factors, divided by 2^e as for a static matrix where its element type is not that of fac.factors, and fac.factors is factorised in place; J === fac.factors is factorised as it is. A DenseSVD divides J by 2^e unconditionally, J === fac.factors too: into fac.factors on the host before gesvd, and into fac.U on a device before the one-sided Jacobi sweep. For DenseSVD, info is 1 on the host where J has a non-finite entry, which is refused before gesvd and leaves U, S and VT unwritten, gesvd's non-convergence code where it fails, or 1 where a singular value of J / 2^e is not finite; on a device it is 1 where the Jacobi sweep reaches its sweep cap without converging or meets a non-finite entry; rank is the numerical rank as for a static matrix. An Inf in a dense J does not throw; the SVD then reports SINGULAR, and the step that the LU or QR factors give is not specified.\n\nA precision marker such as TF32 has no method for a static matrix: the call raises a MethodError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.factorize!!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.ldiv!!","page":"API","text":"ldiv!!(d, fac, r)\n\nSolve A d = r with the factors fac of A, and return d in the element type of r.\n\nFor a static factor (R1), d is nothing and the result is a new SVector. For a dense factor (R3), d is the vector buffer written in place and returned. Where the element type of r is the element type of the factors, the solve runs directly in it. There r is not scaled, unlike in the mixed-type path below: the step overflows on every route where the solution itself exceeds floatmax, and the QR and SVD solves, which form Qᴴ r or Uᴴ r first, can also overflow for a finite r near floatmax whose solution is representable, while the LU solve forms no such product. Where they differ, a finite, nonzero r is scaled by s = maximum(abs, r), converted to the factor type, solved, converted back and multiplied by s (Kelley 2023, eqs. 2.17–2.18). Normalisation prevents overflow from the overall right-hand-side scale in a lower factor precision; entries small relative to the largest entry can still underflow. The scale takes no squares and overflows only where an entry's absolute value does. In the mixed-type path, a zero r returns a zero step without solving, and an Inf or NaN in r gives a non-finite step. In both paths the step is multiplied by 2^-e, the power of two that factorize!! divided J by, in the element type of r. The SVD's solve leaves out the directions that its rank cutoff counted as zero, so that a rank-deficient system gives a finite, truncated step rather than an enormous one; an SVD factor with info ≠ 0 has rank 0 and gives the zero step.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.ldiv!!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.factor_eltype","page":"API","text":"factor_eltype(method, T)\n\nThe element type of the factors of a working element type T: T when the method's factorisation precision is Nothing, the precision TF for a real T, and complex(TF) for a complex T.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.factor_eltype"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.StaticLU","page":"API","text":"StaticLU{N, TF, L}\n\nThe LU factors with partial pivoting of an N × N static matrix, in element type TF: factors holds U on and above the diagonal and the multipliers of the unit lower L below it, ipiv the row exchanged with row k at step k, info the index of the first zero pivot, or 0, and scale_exponent the exponent e of the power of two 2^e that the matrix was divided by. L = N * N makes the SMatrix field concrete, so the factor is isbits.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.StaticLU"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.StaticQR","page":"API","text":"StaticQR{N, TF, L}\n\nThe Householder QR factors of an N × N static matrix, in element type TF: factors holds R on and above the diagonal and below it the reflector vectors w, scaled to w[k] = 1; the reflector of step k is I - τ[k] w wᴴ. info is the index of the first zero diagonal entry of R, or 0, and scale_exponent the exponent e of the power of two 2^e that the matrix was divided by.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.StaticQR"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.StaticSVD","page":"API","text":"StaticSVD{N, TF, R, L}\n\nThe singular value decomposition of an N × N static matrix, in element type TF, computed by the one-sided Jacobi iteration of src/linear/static/svd.jl. factors holds U, V holds the right singular vectors as columns, and σ̂ holds the N singular values of the matrix divided by 2^e, the ones the sweep computed and the solve divides by, in non-increasing order in R = real(TF); singular_values gives those of the matrix as it was passed. rank is the number of singular values above the rank cutoff √N · eps(TF) · σ̂₁; the directions past it are zeroed in U, and the solve of ldiv!! leaves their coordinates out. info is 1 where the sweep reached its cap of 30 sweeps before the columns were orthogonal or a singular value is not finite, and rank is then 0; it is 0 otherwise, also for a rank-deficient matrix, so that returncode reports a failed factorisation alone. scale_exponent is the exponent e of the power of two 2^e that the matrix was divided by.\n\nL = N * N makes the SMatrix fields concrete, so the factor is isbits.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.StaticSVD"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.DenseLU","page":"API","text":"DenseLU{A, P, V, S}\n\nThe LU factors with partial pivoting of a dense n × n matrix, with factors the n × n buffer in the factor element type, ipiv the pivot vector of length n (BlasInt on the host, Int32 on a device), rhs the right-hand-side buffer of the factor element type when that differs from the working element type and nothing otherwise, info the index of the first zero pivot, or 0, scale_exponent the exponent e of the power of two 2^e that the matrix was divided by, and status the 1-element Int32 status buffer on a device array and nothing on the host.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.DenseLU"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.DenseQR","page":"API","text":"DenseQR{A, T, W, V}\n\nThe Householder QR factors of a dense n × n matrix, with factors the n × n buffer in the factor element type (R on and above the diagonal, reflectors below it), τ the reflector scale vector of length n, work the workspace buffer (the LAPACK workspace on the host, and the 1-element Int32 status buffer on a device), rhs the right-hand-side buffer of the factor element type when that differs from the working element type and nothing otherwise, info the index of the first zero diagonal entry of R, or 0, and scale_exponent the exponent e of the power of two 2^e that the matrix was divided by.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.DenseQR"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.DenseSVD","page":"API","text":"DenseSVD{A, S, W, RW, T, V}\n\nThe singular value decomposition factors U * Diagonal(S) * VT of a dense n × n matrix, with factors the n × n Jacobian buffer in the factor element type (which holds V during the device Jacobi sweep), U and VT the n × n unitary factors, S the vector of the singular values σ̂ · 2^e of the matrix in the real factor type, which overflow to Inf where they exceed floatmax, work (the LAPACK workspace on the host, and the 1-element Int32 rotation-flag buffer on a device), rwork (the real LAPACK workspace during gesvd on the host, and the singular values σ̂ of the matrix divided by 2^e in its first n entries, on the host after gesvd and on a device after the sweep) and tmp the workspace buffers, rhs the right-hand-side buffer of the factor element type when that differs from the working element type and nothing otherwise, info 1 where the host matrix has a non-finite entry, which is refused before gesvd, gesvd's non-convergence code, 1 where a host σ̂ is not finite or the device Jacobi sweep reaches its sweep cap without converging or meets a non-finite entry, or 0, rank the number of singular values above the cutoff √n · eps(real(TF)) · σ̂₁, and 0 where info ≠ 0, and scale_exponent the exponent e of the power of two 2^e that the matrix was divided by.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.DenseSVD"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.returncode","page":"API","text":"returncode(fac)\n\nSUCCESS for a factor with info == 0, and SINGULAR otherwise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.returncode"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.exact_solve","page":"API","text":"exact_solve(fac)\n\ntrue where the solve of ldiv!! with the factor fac is the exact solve of the factored matrix, and false for an SVD factor of numerical rank below n, whose solve is the truncated minimum-norm step. Newton measures the slope of a truncated step, because −2φ(0) is the slope of an exact Newton step alone.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.exact_solve"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.singular_values","page":"API","text":"singular_values(fac::StaticSVD)\n\nThe singular values of the matrix that factorize!! factorised, in non-increasing order, as an SVector in real(TF): the stored σ̂ of the matrix divided by 2^e, multiplied by 2^e. A singular value above floatmax(real(TF)) overflows to Inf, although every entry of the matrix is finite; the solve divides by σ̂ and stays finite.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.singular_values"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.jacobi_eigen","page":"API","text":"jacobi_eigen(A)\n\nThe eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the Hermitian matrix A, or of a real symmetric one, by cyclic two-sided Jacobi: the rotations and the round-robin ordering of the Jacobi SVD of SVDFactorization, applied to the rows and the columns of A at once. The eigenvectors are unitary to working precision, which is what the ProjectedSkew exponential algorithm of GeometricOptimizers needs of them. A pair whose off-diagonal entry is at or below eps(real(eltype(A))) * ‖A‖_F is skipped, which is what ends the sweep on a rank-deficient or low-rank matrix, as the SVD's negligible-column guard ends its own.\n\nThe call returns (values, vectors, status): values holds the N eigenvalues in real(eltype(A)), vectors the columns of the unitary V with A = V * Diagonal(values) * V', and status is SUCCESS where the sweep reached its fixed point, or MAXITERS where it reached its cap of 30 sweeps first. All three are isbits, so the call runs inside a kernel.\n\nThe matrix is assumed Hermitian: the call reads its lower triangle and sets the upper triangle to the conjugate of the lower one on entry, so a matrix that is not Hermitian gives the eigensystem of that Hermitian part and not the one of A. A NaN or an Inf entry is not checked: nothing throws, and the sweep reaches its cap for N ≥ 2; at N = 1 there is no pair, so the call returns SUCCESS.\n\nFor a real skew matrix M, the Hermitian matrix is im * M, with the eigenvalues values of im * M and\n\nexp(M) ≈ real(V * Diagonal(exp.(-im .* values)) * V')\n\nto working precision — the ProjectedSkew case, which is what jacobi_eigen exists for.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.jacobi_eigen"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.Static","page":"API","text":"Static(α = 1.0)\n\nThe line search that takes the fixed step α, bounded by the caller's αmax, and evaluates nothing: the common case inside an integrator. It ignores the trial step and returns SUCCESS with φ = NaN.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.Static"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.Backtracking","page":"API","text":"Backtracking(; c₁ = 1e-4, p = 0.5, maxiter = 100)\n\nThe Armijo line search with a safeguarded quadratic and cubic interpolation step. It shrinks the trial step until the sufficient_decrease test\n\nφ(α) - φ(0)  minbigl(0 c_1 α φ(0) + τbigr)\n\nholds, where τ is the roundoff of φ(0). Each backtrack takes the minimiser of the model through φ(0), φ(0) and the last one or two trials, clamped to 01 α p α. A trial whose merit is not finite gives no model; the next trial is then the smaller of 01 α and the geometric mean of α and the step floor. The search stops at the step floor smallest_step, after two trials at steps below sqrtmathrmeps whose merit equals φ(0) bit for bit, or after maxiter trials.\n\nThe slope φ(0) comes from the step kind: -2φ(0) for an ExactStep, one evaluation for a MeasuredSlope, the number itself for a slope passed in. For an InexactStep with residual η the test is Eisenstat and Walker's r(x + α d)  1 - c_1 (1 - η(α)) r with η(α) = 1 - α + α η, and the model slope is -2(1 - η) φ(0).\n\nThe model is built from differences of the merit divided by φ(0), and the cubic step uses the form that does not cancel on a near-quadratic merit, so the trials do not depend on the scale of the merit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.Backtracking"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.Bisection","page":"API","text":"Bisection(; αmax = 65536.0, maxiter = 100)\n\nThe line search that bisects φ for a minimiser of the merit along the line. It brackets from the anchor: the lower end starts at 0, where φ(0)  0, and the upper end at the trial step, doubled while φ  0, up to the smaller of its own αmax and the caller's. So the bracket always holds a sign change from - to +: a minimum, never a maximum. A φ that is not finite counts as not descending, so the bracket never grows into a region where the merit is not finite.\n\nIt then bisects the bracket. While the bracket spans more than a factor 2, the next trial is the geometric mean of its ends, with a lower end of 0 taken as the step floor smallest_step; after that it is the middle. It stops when the width is below sqrtmathrmeps times the upper end, or when the lower end is still 0 and the upper end is within a factor 2 of the floor. So the number of trials grows only with log_2 log_2 of the ratio of the trial step to the floor, and it stays bounded for αmax = Inf. While the lower end stays at 0, a search from the trial step α costs at most 3 + log_2 log_2(α  mathrmfloatmin(R)) evaluations, because the floor is at least floatmin(R): 10 in Float32 and 13 in Float64 for α = 1.\n\nIt returns the lower end of the bracket, where φ is finite and negative, its upper end if the lower end is 0, or the ceiling if φ is still negative there, and classifies it by the merit there: one evaluation of φ after one φ per trial.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.Bisection"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.StrongWolfe","page":"API","text":"StrongWolfe(; c₁ = 1e-4, c₂ = 0.9, αmax = 65536.0, maxiter = 100)\n\nThe line search for a step with the strong Wolfe conditions\n\nφ(α) - φ(0)  c_1 α φ(0) qquad φ(α)  c_2 φ(0)\n\n0  c_1  c_2  1: Nocedal and Wright's Algorithms 3.5 and 3.6. The first condition is the sufficient_decrease test without a round-off allowance. The bracketing phase doubles the trial step up to the smaller of αmax and the caller's ceiling until a trial fails the first condition, does not decrease the merit, or has φ  0. The zoom phase then takes the minimiser of the cubic through the merit and its derivative at both ends of the bracket, or of the quadratic through both merits and the derivative at the lower end where the upper end has no derivative. The trial is safeguarded to the inner 80 % of the bracket, and it is the middle after a trial that shrinks the bracket by less than a factor 0.66, as in Moré and Thuente. The lower end is the trial with the lowest merit that meets the first condition, up to the roundoff of the larger of two merits: a trial within it of the lower end ties with it, and the sign of φ at the trial decides the side of the minimiser. A zoom trial that meets both conditions is accepted whatever its merit.\n\nφ is evaluated only at a trial that meets the first condition and, in the bracketing phase, decreases the merit: a trial that fails costs one φ, a trial that passes one φ and one φ.\n\nIt returns SUCCESS only for a step that meets both conditions and decreases the merit by more than its round-off. Every other search returns its lowest trial that meets the first condition, or else its last trial. At the ceiling, at a collapsed bracket or at the step floor smallest_step the code is STALLED if the merit is at its round-off floor and LINESEARCH_FAILED otherwise; after maxiter trials it is LINESEARCH_FAILED.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.StrongWolfe"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.DogLeg","page":"API","text":"DogLeg(; radius = nothing)\n\nThe trust-region globalization of Newton on the dogleg curve: Newton(; globalization = DogLeg()). It is not a LineSearch: it needs the Jacobian, its factor and a radius that lives from one iteration to the next, so Newton holds the radius in its state and runs the trial loop with its own Jacobian.\n\nThe defaults are Procedure 4.1 of Pawlowski, Simonis, Walker and Shadid (2008). The step is the Newton step when it lies in the radius δ, else the Cauchy step along -J^* F scaled to δ when the Cauchy point lies outside δ, else the point of norm δ on the segment from the Cauchy point to the Newton step. J is the matrix that was factorised, regularisation included. A trial is accepted when the predicted reduction mathrmpred = F(x) - F(x) + J s is positive and the actual reduction mathrmared = F(x) - F(x + s) satisfies mathrmared  10^-4 mathrmpred, with unsquared norms; a rejected trial shrinks δ to max(δ4 10^-6) and chooses again on the same curve, with no new Jacobian or solve. So a rejected Newton step that lies well inside δ is tried again, at one evaluation of F each time, until δ falls below its norm. A rejection at δ = 10^-6 returns LINESEARCH_FAILED with the iterate kept. After an accepted step with ratio ρ = mathrmared  mathrmpred, ρ  01 sets δ to max(s^N 10^-6) when the Newton step s^N is shorter than δ and to max(δ4 10^-6) otherwise, and ρ  075 on the boundary sets it to min(4δ 10^10). An accepted step other than the whole Newton step sets limited in its StepInfo: the radius or the Cauchy point, not the distance to a root, sets its norm, so the solve applies only the residual test after it.\n\nradius is the initial radius. It must be positive and finite as given; DogLeg stores it converted to float(typeof(radius)), and ToReal to the iterate's real type, clamped to 10^-6 10^10 after each conversion. With nothing, the first step starts the radius at the norm of the first Newton step, clamped the same way, except that a norm below 10^-6 starts it at 2 cdot 10^-6, as Procedure 4.1 does. A failed factor, or a Newton step whose norm is zero or not finite, gives no Newton step: the step is then the Cauchy point, cut to the radius when it lies outside, and a first radius starts at the length of the Cauchy point, by the same rule. The Cauchy direction is formed from the unit residual, tilde g = J^* F  F, so no norm of J^* F occurs, and a common scale of F and J leaves the Cauchy point unchanged up to rounding, as long as the norm of tilde g is finite. Where the Cauchy point is needed and tilde g is zero or its norm is not finite, or J tilde g  tilde g is zero or its norm is not finite, the step returns STALLED with the iterate kept. At F(x) = 0 the step returns SUCCESS with the iterate and the radius kept, and makes no trial.\n\nDogLeg takes no factor reuse: Newton(; globalization = DogLeg(), reuse = k) raises an ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.DogLeg"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.NonlinearSolver","page":"API","text":"NonlinearSolver(prob, x, method, options, state, p)\n\nA preallocated nonlinear solver for NonlinearProblem prob with method and Options options, holding the workspace and AD preparation in state, the iterate x and the parameters p, in the fields x::X and p::Q. It keeps the element type T and the length n of x and the types X of x and Q of p, which solve! checks. Build it with init(prob, x₀, method, p; options = nothing, kwargs...) and solve with solve!(solver) or solve!(solver, x, p).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.NonlinearSolver"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CommonSolve.init","page":"API","text":"init(prob::NonlinearProblem, x₀, method, p = nothing; options = nothing, kwargs...)\n\nAllocate and prepare a NonlinearSolver for prob and method from the initial iterate x₀ and the parameters p, with the arguments of solve, so that solve!(init(args...; kwargs...)) returns what solve(args...; kwargs...) returns. The solver holds a copy of an array x₀, so init and solve!(solver) do not mutate x₀; it holds a StaticArray or a Number x₀ and p themselves. Passing both options and keyword tolerances raises an ArgumentError, and so does an options that is not an Options{real(eltype(x₀))}.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommonSolve.init"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CommonSolve.solve","page":"API","text":"solve(prob::NonlinearProblem, x₀, method, p = nothing; options = nothing, kwargs...)\n\nSolve NonlinearProblem prob from x₀ with method and parameters p, and return (x, status) where status is a SolverStatus. For an R3 array iterate x₀, solve copies x₀ so the caller's initial guess is not mutated. solve(args...; kwargs...) returns what solve!(init(args...; kwargs...)) returns, without building a NonlinearSolver.\n\nsolve adapts method to real(eltype(x₀)) with ToReal, builds Options in eltype(x₀) from kwargs... or takes options, calls init_state, and delegates to solve!!. Passing both options and keyword tolerances raises an ArgumentError, and so does an options that is not an Options{real(eltype(x₀))}: a Float32 iterate takes Options(Float32; …), because the default tolerances of Options(Float64) are below what a Float32 solve reaches. The residual norm rnorm is finite up to floatmax, but the merit ‖F‖² of a line search overflows to Inf above ‖F‖ = sqrt(floatmax), and a solve with a line search then returns NONFINITE.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommonSolve.solve"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CommonSolve.solve!","page":"API","text":"solve!(solver::NonlinearSolver)\nsolve!(solver::NonlinearSolver, x, p = solver.p)\n\nSolve solver.prob, using the preallocated workspace and AD preparation in solver, store the solution in solver.x, and return (solver.x, status) where status is a SolverStatus. solve!(solver) solves from solver.x with solver.p, the iterate and the parameters that init or the last solve!(solver, x, p) stored, and writes the solution into solver.x when it is an array. solve!(solver, x, p) stores x in solver.x and p in solver.p, then solves from x with p; an array x receives the solution in place and stays in solver.x. Without p, it solves with the parameters that the solver holds. An x whose element type or length differs from the iterate of init raises an ArgumentError, and so do an x of another type and a p of another type than those of init. A refused call changes neither solver nor x.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommonSolve.solve!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.solve_batch!","page":"API","text":"solve_batch!(X::AbstractVector{<:Union{SVector, Number}},\n             S::AbstractVector{SolverStatus{R}},\n             prob::NonlinearProblem,\n             method,\n             p = nothing;\n             order = nothing,\n             options = nothing,\n             kwargs...) -> S\n\nsolve_batch!(X::AbstractVector{<:Union{SVector, Number}},\n             prob::NonlinearProblem,\n             method,\n             p = nothing;\n             order = nothing,\n             options = nothing,\n             kwargs...) -> NamedTuple{(:SUCCESS, :STALLED, :MAXITERS, :SINGULAR, :NONFINITE, :LINESEARCH_FAILED), NTuple{6, Int}}\n\nSolve a batch of independent small nonlinear systems in place on get_backend(X), assigning one work-item per system.\n\nEach element X[idx] is the initial guess for system idx and is overwritten with its final iterate. Wrap an indexable per-system parameter collection in PerSystem so system idx receives p.data[idx]; any other p is shared across all systems. When order is a permutation of 1:length(X), work-item i solves system idx = order[i]; any other order throws. An order that is not isbits needs a method of get_backend, and an order on another backend than X is copied to the backend of X.\n\nThe first form writes each system's SolverStatus into S[idx] and returns S (requiring R === real(eltype(eltype(X))) and get_backend(S) == get_backend(X)). The second form allocates a 1-byte-per-system ReturnCode buffer on get_backend(X), copies the codes once to the host after kernel completion, and returns the six outcome counts as a NamedTuple.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.solve_batch!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.solve!!","page":"API","text":"solve!!(state, prob::NonlinearProblem, x₀, method, p = nothing, options = Options(eltype(x₀)))\n\nRun the nonlinear solver loop from x₀ using state = init_state(method, prob, x₀, p) and return (x, status, state). solve!! is the in-place and kernel entry point: inside a KernelAbstractions.@kernel, a caller prepares state₀ = init_state(m, prob, x₀, p) on the host and calls solve!!(state₀, prob, x_i, m, p_i, opt) in each work-item.\n\nAt the start of each call, solve!! evaluates Fx = residual!!(residual_buffer(state), prob, x₀, p) and fnorm₀ = rnorm(Fx), and calls start!! once, which resets the per-solve state of the method (the reuse age and the stalled flag under factor reuse, and the radius under DogLeg). Every return goes through finish!!, and solve!! returns the triple that finish!! returns.\n\nWhen fnorm₀ is not finite — an entry of Fx is not finite, or ‖F‖ is above floatmax — it returns NONFINITE with 0 iterations. When the residual test of Options already holds at 0 iterations, it returns SUCCESS. When options.max_iterations == 0, it returns MAXITERS with 0 iterations. options must be an Options{real(eltype(x₀))}; any other raises an ArgumentError.\n\nInside the loop, each trip calls step!! and updates status via record. SINGULAR and NONFINITE return immediately. A LINESEARCH_FAILED step whose stepnorm is zero (meaning the failed line-search trial did not decrease the merit) stops immediately with LINESEARCH_FAILED; one that decreased the merit keeps its new iterate, increments status.step_failures, and continues. Only the residual test returns SUCCESS. When it fails after a step that is neither STALLED nor limited, a step test that holds returns STALLED at once, and so does a SUCCESS step of zero norm, which leaves the iterate where the next step would start; min_iterations bounds both. After a STALLED step only the residual test applies, and options.max_stalls consecutive STALLED steps return STALLED. After a step whose limited is set only the residual test applies too, because a radius, not the distance to a root, sets its norm; such a step is not a stall. Reaching options.max_iterations without convergence returns MAXITERS.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.solve!!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.PerSystem","page":"API","text":"PerSystem(data)\n\nWrap an indexable collection of per-system parameters so solve_batch! passes data[i] to system i. Any parameter value not wrapped in PerSystem — a number, a tuple, an SVector, or a plain AbstractVector — is shared unchanged across every system in the batch.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.PerSystem"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.Newton","page":"API","text":"Newton(; linsolve = LUFactorization(), globalization = Static(), jacobian = nothing,\n         refinement = NoRefinement(), regularization = 0, reuse = nothing)\n\nNewton's method for NonlinearProblem, with linear solver linsolve (LUFactorization or QRFactorization in R1), globalization globalization (Static, Backtracking, Bisection, StrongWolfe or the trust region DogLeg), Jacobian selector jacobian, iterative refinement refinement (NoRefinement), diagonal regularisation regularization, and factor-reuse period reuse.\n\nFields\n\nlinsolve::L: the linear-solver method.\nglobalization::G: the globalization method.\njacobian::J: nothing (use prob.J when present, or AutoForwardDiff() otherwise), an AbstractADType, or a custom Jacobian callable J(x, p). Adapting Newton with ToReal (adapt(ToReal{R}(), m)) converts floating-point values captured inside a jacobian closure to R, while a function with no captured floats passes through unchanged.\nrefinement::RF: the refinement setting (NoRefinement).\nregularization::R: non-negative finite diagonal shift μ  0 added as J + μ I before factorisation when μ > 0.\nreuse::K: nothing (K = Nothing, factorise at every step) or Int32(k) (K = Int32, reuse the factor for up to k consecutive steps, refreshing early after a STALLED step). A line search along a reused factor that fails without a step is retried once, in the same step, from a fresh factor. A DogLeg globalization takes no reuse: its model needs the Jacobian of the current iterate, and the constructor raises an ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.Newton"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.Picard","page":"API","text":"Picard(; damping = 1, globalization = Static())\n\nDamped fixed-point (Picard) iteration for NonlinearProblem, with search direction d = -mathrmdampingF(x p) and globalization globalization (Static, Backtracking, Bisection or StrongWolfe).\n\nFields\n\ndamping::D: positive finite damping factor omega  0, stored in floating-point.\nglobalization::G: the globalization method. Any line search uses MeasuredSlope.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.Picard"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.NoRefinement","page":"API","text":"NoRefinement()\n\nThe default iterative-refinement setting of Newton, which returns the linear solve's step unchanged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.NoRefinement"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.prepare_ad","page":"API","text":"prepare_ad(backend, prob, r, x, p)\n\nBuild the AD preparation for the residual of prob at an iterate like x, with a residual buffer like r and a representative parameter set p, and return the object that jacobian!! and jvp!! dispatch on. The values of p, and the parameter object itself, may be replaced on every call.\n\nAutoForwardDiff() gives a ChunkedForwardDiff on every array type. Any other back end gives a DIJacobian on a CPU iterate, and raises ArgumentError on a device array iterate: DifferentiationInterface's jacobian! raises \"Scalar indexing is disallowed\" on an MtlArray, a CuArray and a JLArray. The iterate must be a vector.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.prepare_ad"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.DIJacobian","page":"API","text":"DIJacobian\n\nThe AD preparation of the R3 path through DifferentiationInterface: the back end, DI's prepared jacobian! and pushforward!, and a residual buffer. prepare_ad builds it. The parameters are a context and not a differentiated argument, so each call wraps them in DI's Constant.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.DIJacobian"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.ChunkedForwardDiff","page":"API","text":"ChunkedForwardDiff{N, Tg}\n\nThe AD preparation of the R3 path of AutoForwardDiff: N Jacobian columns per residual evaluation, in one pass of ForwardDiff.Dual{Tg, T, N} arithmetic. Tg is the caller's tag, or this solver's own where the caller named none, and the same tag serves the Jacobian and the JVP.\n\nThe four fields are the dual buffers: xdual and rdual carry N partials for jacobian!!, xdual1 and rdual1 one partial for jvp!!. A complex iterate gives Complex{ForwardDiff.Dual{Tg, real(T), N}} buffers, so that a holomorphic residual is differentiated in its complex argument.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.ChunkedForwardDiff"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.JacobianFunction","page":"API","text":"JacobianFunction(f)\n\nThe Jacobian source of a method that carries an operator of its own: f(x, p) is that operator, and jacobian!! calls it in place of the problem's J. The method's operator is not the derivative of the residual, so it serves jacobian!! and never jvp!!.\n\nInternal: wrap the operator in this type to pass it as the source of the R1 jacobian!! call. It holds a function and nothing else, so it is isbits exactly when that function is.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.JacobianFunction"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.JacobianMatrixJVP","page":"API","text":"JacobianMatrixJVP(J)\n\nThe R3 JVP preparation when the problem provides an analytic Jacobian prob.J(J, x, p): a preallocated n × n matrix buffer J into which jvp!! evaluates prob.J(J, x, p) and multiplies by v via mul!!. Because this buffer is distinct from Newton's Jacobian buffer, evaluating a line-search slope under Jacobian reuse (reuse > 1) leaves the held Newton Jacobian untouched.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.JacobianMatrixJVP"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.jacobian!!","page":"API","text":"jacobian!!(J, prep, prob, x, p)\n\nThe Jacobian of the residual of prob at the iterate x, for the parameters p, in the storage of J, and return it.\n\nprep is the Jacobian source. In R1 the output slot J is nothing: nothing calls the problem's J(x, p), an AutoForwardDiff runs tagged ForwardDiff and returns an SMatrix in the element type of a vector iterate or a Number for a Number iterate, and a JacobianFunction calls the method's own function. factorize!! converts the result to the factorisation precision TF. In R3 J is an n × n buffer in the element type of x: nothing calls prob.J(J, x, p), a JacobianFunction calls s.f(J, x, p), a DIJacobian runs DifferentiationInterface's prepared jacobian!, and a ChunkedForwardDiff runs the own chunked forward mode. The preparation selects the code.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.jacobian!!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.jvp!!","page":"API","text":"jvp!!(Jv, prep, prob, x, v, p)\n\nThe Jacobian–vector product J(x, p) * v of the residual of prob, in the storage of Jv, and return it.\n\nWith an AD source, one pushforward and exactly one evaluation of the residual make the merit derivative φ′(α) = F(x + αd)ᵀ J(x + αd) d of a line search cost one directional derivative rather than n. With the source nothing or a JacobianMatrixJVP, the call evaluates the problem's J instead of F.\n\nprep is the JVP source from the problem, never from a method's operator, and it selects the code exactly as in jacobian!!. In R1 the output slot Jv is nothing and the call returns a new value: nothing gives J(x, p) * v from the problem's J, and an AutoForwardDiff one dual evaluation of F. The R1 direction has the element type and the length of the iterate, as the line-search slope does: a direction of another element type is a host MethodError. In R3 Jv is a buffer and prep is a JacobianMatrixJVP, a DIJacobian or a ChunkedForwardDiff.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.jvp!!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.init_state","page":"API","text":"init_state(method, prob::NonlinearProblem, x, p)\n\nBuild the state of method for NonlinearProblem prob at the initial iterate x and parameters p: its workspace, its AD preparation and its rule state, in one concrete type per method. In R1 (SVector, MVector or Number), init_state is allocation-free and returns an isbits state when prob and method are isbits.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.init_state"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.residual_buffer","page":"API","text":"residual_buffer(state)\n\nThe slot that solve!! evaluates F(x₀) into: the residual workspace of state for an array iterate, and nothing for a value iterate. The default is nothing, which lets residual!! return a new value.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.residual_buffer"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.step!!","page":"API","text":"step!!(state, method, prob::NonlinearProblem, x, Fx, p)\n\nCompute one nonlinear solver step from (x, Fx) and return (x₊, F₊, state₊, info) where info is a StepInfo.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.step!!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.start!!","page":"API","text":"start!!(state, method, prob::NonlinearProblem, x, Fx, p, fnorm₀) -> state\n\nPrepare state for a solve from x, with residual Fx and its norm fnorm₀, and return the state that the first step reads. solve!! calls it once, before its stopping test at 0 iterations, so a state reused across solves starts each solve afresh. The default returns state; Newton resets its rule state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.start!!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.finish!!","page":"API","text":"finish!!(state, method, prob::NonlinearProblem, x, Fx, status) -> (x, status, state)\n\nClose a solve that ends at x, with residual Fx and status, and return the triple that solve!! returns. solve!! calls it at every return. The default returns (x, status, state). A method may return another iterate; it then sets status.fnorm itself, and for an array iterate it writes that iterate into x and returns x.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.finish!!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.init_globalization","page":"API","text":"init_globalization(g, x)\n\nAllocate the workspace named tuple for globalization method g at iterate x through buffer(x): (; xt, Ft) for Static, (; xt, Ft, Jv) for a LineSearch, and (; g̃, ĝ, Jg, s, xt, Ft) for DogLeg. In R1 every slot is nothing; in R3 each slot is a preallocated vector like x.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.init_globalization"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.LineFunction","page":"API","text":"LineFunction(prob, x, Fx, d, p, jvpprep, xt = nothing, Ft = nothing, Jv = nothing)\n\nThe line function along the ray from x in the direction d for NonlinearProblem prob with parameters p, Jacobian-vector preparation jvpprep, and workspace slots xt, Ft and Jv; Fx is the residual at x. It is a callable struct, not a subtype of Function, so isbits holds when its fields are isbits.\n\nφ(lf, α) advances x along d by α through update_iterate!!, evaluates the residual through residual!!, and returns its merit norm2, F_2^2. φ′(lf, α) evaluates the derivative of the merit, 2mathrmRelangle F J drangle, through jvp!! and rdot. A line search along lf returns the residual at its step in its LineSearchResult, and Fx for a step it did not evaluate. Calling lf(α) delegates to φ(lf, α).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.LineFunction"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.globalize!!","page":"API","text":"globalize!!(g, ls, prob, x, Fx, d, p, jvpprep, stepkind = MeasuredSlope())\n\nAdvance x along d under the globalization method ls using workspace g and return (x₊, F₊, g, info) where info is a StepInfo.\n\nFor Static, globalize!! takes the fixed step ls.α through update_iterate!! and evaluates residual!! once, without a line-search loop. When the step d or the new residual norm is not finite, it returns (x, Fx, g, StepInfo{R}(NONFINITE, rnorm(Fx), abs(ls.α) * rnorm(d), 0, false, false)) without advancing the iterate or evaluating F at a non-finite trial point.\n\nFor Backtracking, Bisection and StrongWolfe, globalize!! returns NONFINITE immediately when rnorm(d) is not finite; otherwise it builds a LineFunction and calls linesearch from α = 1. An accepted step returns the residual that the search evaluated at res.α, so it costs no further evaluation of F. A LINESEARCH_FAILED result whose φ is finite and strictly less than φ₀ = norm2(Fx) takes the step res.α and reports failures = 1; otherwise the iterate stays at x with stepnorm = 0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.globalize!!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.LineSearch","page":"API","text":"LineSearch{R}\n\nThe supertype of the line searches Static, Backtracking, Bisection and StrongWolfe, whose numbers are of the real type R. A line search is a method: it is isbits, it holds numbers and, except Static, an Int32 cap and nothing else, and linesearch runs it.\n\nEvery line search keeps six contracts:\n\nIt never throws. A constructor checks its parameters on the host and raises an ArgumentError; linesearch raises nothing.\nIt returns α  0.\nIt reports through the code of its LineSearchResult and never logs.\nA non-finite or ascending anchor is reported, not searched: NONFINITE and LINESEARCH_FAILED. A stationary anchor, φ(0) = 0, is STALLED. Static is the exception: it reads no anchor and returns its step.\nIts number of evaluations does not depend on the scale of the merit.\nIt returns α  α_max, for the caller's αmax and for its own.\n\nIt never evaluates the merit at α = 0: the caller passes φ(0), which is the residual it already has.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.LineSearch"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.linesearch","page":"API","text":"linesearch(ls::LineSearch{R}, lf, step, φ₀::R, α::R, αmax::R = R(Inf))\n\nRun the line search ls along the line function lf from the trial step α, and return a LineSearchResult{R}. φ₀ is the merit at α = 0, which the caller has; step is an ExactStep, an InexactStep{R}, a MeasuredSlope or the slope φ(0)R itself; αmax is the caller's ceiling on the step. A trial step that is not positive or not finite is replaced by 1, and one below the step floor smallest_step is raised to it.\n\nA non-positive or NaN αmax is a caller error. The search then evaluates nothing and returns LINESEARCH_FAILED with the trial step, bounded by the ceiling of the method.\n\nThis is the one entry point: it applies the ceiling, the trial step and the anchor checks, then runs the loop of the method. Each loop has exactly ls.maxiter trips with a done flag, so that the threads of a warp stay together in a kernel; a trip after the flag is set evaluates nothing. Static has no loop.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.linesearch"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.φ","page":"API","text":"φ(lf, α)\n\nThe merit of the line function lf at the step α, φ(α) = r(x + α d)^2, in the real type of α. A line search sees a line function only through this and φ′, and through merit_and_residual and anchor_residual, whose fallbacks call φ and return nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.φ"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.φ′","page":"API","text":"φ′(lf, α)\n\nThe derivative dφdα of the merit of the line function lf at the step α: one Jacobian–vector product.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.φ′"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.ExactStep","page":"API","text":"ExactStep()\n\nThe step kind of a direction from a fresh Jacobian and an exact linear solve. For the merit φ = r^2 it gives φ(0) = -2φ(0), so no line search evaluates φ(0). For φ(0)  mathrmfloatmax(R)2 that slope overflows, and the search reports NONFINITE.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.ExactStep"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.InexactStep","page":"API","text":"InexactStep(η)\n\nThe step kind of a direction d with the linear residual r + J d  η r, 0  η  1. It gives only φ(0)  -2(1 - η) φ(0) (Eisenstat and Walker 1996, (1.2)). Along α d the linear residual is (1 - α) r + α (r + J d), so its bound is η(α) = 1 - α + α η. Backtracking tests r(x + α d)  1 - c_1 (1 - η(α)) r with this η(α), which for α  1 is the update η  1 - θ (1 - η) of each backtrack by θ, and evaluates no φ. A step with η(α)  1 promises no decrease and is rejected. Bisection and StrongWolfe need the true slope and evaluate φ(0). The constructor checks nothing, so that solver code can build one in a kernel: a search given an η outside 0 1) evaluates nothing and returns LINESEARCH_FAILED. Static reads no step kind and returns its step.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.InexactStep"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.MeasuredSlope","page":"API","text":"MeasuredSlope()\n\nThe step kind of any other descent direction, such as one from a reused Jacobian: every line search except Static evaluates φ(0) once. A caller that already has φ(0) passes the number itself as the step kind, and no search evaluates it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.MeasuredSlope"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.LineSearchResult","page":"API","text":"LineSearchResult{R <: Real, Q}\n\nThe result of one linesearch. It is isbits when the residual is. LineSearchResult{R}(α, φ, code, evaluations) builds one with the residual nothing.\n\nFields\n\nα::R: the step, 0  α  α_max.\nφ::R: the merit at α, or NaN if the search did not evaluate it there.\ncode::ReturnCode: SUCCESS for an accepted step that decreases the merit by more than its round-off, and for the step of Static, which evaluates nothing; STALLED for a step that changes the merit by no more than its round-off, or a stationary anchor; NONFINITE for a non-finite anchor; LINESEARCH_FAILED for anything else: an ascending anchor, a spent cap, an increase of the merit, an invalid αmax, or a StrongWolfe step without the curvature condition. φ tells the caller whether the step of a failure still decreases the merit.\nevaluations::Int32: the evaluations of φ and φ′ together. A StrongWolfe search whose trials share one residual buffer evaluates F once more at a result step that is not its last trial, to restore residual; that evaluation is not counted.\nresidual::Q: the residual at α that gave φ; the residual at α = 0 where φ is NaN; nothing for a line function with no residual.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.LineSearchResult"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.roundoff","page":"API","text":"roundoff(φ₀)\n\nThe round-off resolution τ = 4mathrmeps(R)φ(0) of the merit, four units of relative round-off. A step that changes the merit by no more than τ is at the round-off floor of the merit. It is proportional to φ(0), not a count of ulps of φ(0), so that it and the step floor smallest_step scale with the merit and do not jump by a factor 2 at a power of two. For a subnormal φ(0), where the product underflows, it is at least four of the smallest subnormals, so it is never 0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.roundoff"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.smallest_step","page":"API","text":"smallest_step(d₀, τ)\n\nThe step floor τ  φ(0) of every search: below it the decrease that the slope predicts, α φ(0), is smaller than the round-off τ, so no trial can show it. It is at least floatmin(R), so that no search tries a step of 0 when the quotient underflows.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.smallest_step"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.sufficient_decrease","page":"API","text":"sufficient_decrease(φα, φ₀, demand, τ)\n\nThe Armijo test on the difference, φ(α) - φ(0)  min(0 mathrmdemand + τ), where demand is the negative decrease the test demands and τ the round-off allowance. The difference keeps a demand below one ulp of φ(0), which φ(0) + mathrmdemand would round away; the min lets τ lower the demand, never accept an increase. StrongWolfe passes τ = 0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.sufficient_decrease"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.classify","page":"API","text":"classify(φα, φ₀, τ)\n\nThe code of an accepted step: SUCCESS for a finite decrease by more than τ, STALLED for a change by no more than τ, and LINESEARCH_FAILED for an increase by more than τ or a merit that is not finite.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.classify"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.backtrack_step","page":"API","text":"backtrack_step(φ₀, d₀, α, φα, αp, φp, p)\n\nThe next trial step after the rejected trial α with merit φα; αp and φp are the trial before it, and αp is NaN on the first backtrack. The model of φ is a quadratic through φ(0), φ(0) = d_0 and φ(α), then a cubic through φ(α_p) as well, both in the merit divided by d_0. The cubic minimiser is 1  (b + sqrtb^2 + 3a) for b  0, which does not cancel as a  0. The result is clamped to 01 α p α; a model without a minimiser gives p α, and a merit that is not finite at α gives 01 α.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.backtrack_step"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.zoom_step","page":"API","text":"zoom_step(a, φa, da, b, φb, db)\n\nThe minimiser of the cubic through the merit φa, φb and its derivative da, db at the two ends a and b of a bracket (Nocedal and Wright, (3.59)), clamped to the inner 80 % of the bracket. The discriminant is scaled by the largest of θ, d_a, d_b, as in Moré and Thuente, so it neither overflows nor depends on the scale of the merit. A cubic without a minimiser gives the middle of the bracket. Without a finite db it is the minimiser of the quadratic through φa, da and φb.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.zoom_step"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.LinearMethod","page":"API","text":"LinearMethod{TF}\n\nThe supertype of the linear-solver methods. TF is the factorisation precision: Nothing for the working type of the problem, a real float type, or a PrecisionMarker. It is a type parameter and not a field, because a type stored in a field is not isbits, and a method must enter a kernel.\n\nTF is a real type for a complex problem too: LUFactorization(Float32) factorises a ComplexF64 Jacobian in ComplexF32.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.LinearMethod"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.jacobi_rotation","page":"API","text":"jacobi_rotation(α, β, γ)\n\nThe rotation (cs, sn) that makes the Hermitian 2 × 2 matrix [α γ; γ̄ β] diagonal, with real α and β: with G = [cs sn; -conj(sn) cs], the product Gᴴ [α γ; γ̄ β] G is diagonal, and θ with tan θ = t is at most π/4 in modulus, which is what makes the sweep converge rather than merely rearrange.\n\nγ must not be zero: the phase of the rotation is γ / |γ|.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.jacobi_rotation"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.ToReal","page":"API","text":"ToReal{R}()\n\nThe Adapt rule that converts every AbstractFloat in a method tree to R. Applied on the host with R = real(eltype(x)), it lets a method written with Float64 literals run in a Float32 kernel that never sees a Float64. An integer, a type and a function pass through unchanged.\n\nEach method type that holds a float adds Adapt.@adapt_structure for itself.\n\njulia> GeometricSolvers.Adapt.adapt(GeometricSolvers.ToReal{Float32}(), (0.7, Int32(3), sin))\n(0.7f0, 3, sin)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.ToReal"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.parameters","page":"API","text":"parameters(p, i::Integer)\n\nReturn the parameter value for system i: p.data[i] when p isa PerSystem, and p unchanged otherwise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.parameters"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.record","page":"API","text":"record(status::SolverStatus{R}, info::StepInfo{R})\n\nFold the result of one step into the status: count the iteration, take the code and the norms of the step, add its failures, and keep promoted once it is set.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.record"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.converged","page":"API","text":"converged(options::Options{R}, status::SolverStatus{R}, fnorm₀::R, xnorm::R)\n\nThe stopping test of Options at status, for a start with residual norm fnorm₀ and an iterate of norm xnorm. It holds once min_iterations steps are taken and either the residual test or, after a step of nonzero norm, the step test holds. At 0 iterations it is the test before the first step: the relative residual test then holds only for F(x₀) = 0, so a start passes it through f_abstol. A zero step is no progress, so it never passes the step test.\n\nsolve!! returns SUCCESS only through the residual test: a step test that holds while the residual test fails returns STALLED.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.converged"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.isconverged","page":"API","text":"isconverged(status::SolverStatus)\nisconverged(counts::NamedTuple{(:SUCCESS, :STALLED, :MAXITERS, :SINGULAR, :NONFINITE, :LINESEARCH_FAILED)})\n\nWhether the solve ended with SUCCESS, or whether every system in a batch return-code count summary ended with SUCCESS (counts.SUCCESS == sum(counts)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.isconverged"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.isstalled","page":"API","text":"isstalled(status::SolverStatus)\n\nWhether the solve ended with STALLED.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.isstalled"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.norm2","page":"API","text":"norm2(a)\n\nThe squared Euclidean norm sum_i a_i^2 in real(eltype(a)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.norm2"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.rnorm","page":"API","text":"rnorm(a)\n\nThe Euclidean norm sqrtsum_i a_i^2 in real(eltype(a)).\n\nWhere norm2(a) is finite and at least length(a) * floatmin(R), it is sqrt(norm2(a)), one reduction. Otherwise it scales the entries by 2^-e, where 2^e is the power of two of the largest a_i (of the largest real or imaginary part for a complex a), so that it neither overflows where the norm is finite nor underflows to zero where the norm is positive. That path takes two more reductions, the largest entry and the sum, and sums in at least Float32. Scaling can round the squares of entries much smaller than the largest to zero, which changes the result in the last bits at most. A NaN entry gives NaN, an Inf entry and no NaN gives Inf. Where real(eltype(a)) is no AbstractFloat, such as an integer or a ForwardDiff.Dual, it is the unscaled sqrt(norm2(a)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.rnorm"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GeometricSolvers.rdot","page":"API","text":"rdot(a, b)\n\nThe real part of the inner product, mathrmRe sum_i bara_i b_i, in real(promote_type(eltype(a), eltype(b))). That is the dot product of the real vector space under a complex T, which a merit derivative needs. Arguments with different axes raise a DimensionMismatch.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.rdot"},{"category":"section","location":"#GeometricSolvers","page":"Home","text":"Documentation for GeometricSolvers.\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers"},{"category":"module","location":"#GeometricSolvers.GeometricSolvers","page":"Home","text":"Solvers with the problem/method/solver interface of SimpleSolvers, built so that the same solvers run on the CPU and on CUDA, ROCm and Metal devices through KernelAbstractions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.GeometricSolvers"}]
}
